            <ArticleTitle>Delayed-matching-to-place Task in a Dry Maze to Measure Spatial Working Memory in Mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e2389</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.21769/BioProtoc.2389</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The delayed-matching-to-place (DMP) dry maze test is a variant of DMP water maze (Steele and Morris, 1999; Faizi et al., 2012) which measures spatial working/episodic-like learning and memory that depends on both hippocampal and cortical functions (Wang and Morris, 2010; Euston et al., 2012). Using this test we can detect normal aging related spatial working memory decline, as well as trauma induced working memory deficits. Furthermore, we recently reported that fractionated whole brain irradiation does not affect working memory in mice (Feng et al., 2016). Here we describe the experimental setup and procedures of this behavioral test.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
